{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch04.s02",
  "inputs_hash": "773e5587014384480e85ecd98c3e44da635740f3923c1835ed7accc3112326b4",
  "output_hash": "4604e6c14055b778e0a1bb89bd231a518d67e82d9bb3312ec466c01aa7ee6ced",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch04.s02",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch04.s02.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.01",
            "text": "How does raw text get tied to the fixed numbers a GPT model is built around, and why do those numbers matter before any code runs?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.02",
            "text": "A GPT configuration includes parameters such as vocabulary size, context length, embedding dimension, number of attention heads, number of transformer layers, dropout rate, and whether query, key, and value use a bias term.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@320",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@324",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@326",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@328",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@330",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@363",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@387",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@398",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@411",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@416",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@421"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.03",
            "text": "The vocabulary size is the number of tokens in the vocabulary.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@330",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@333"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.04",
            "text": "GPT-2 has a vocabulary of size 50,257.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@642",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@645",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@654",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@352",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@355",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@356"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.05",
            "text": "The tokenizer used here is tiktoken, and it uses byte pair encoding to create a vocabulary of tokens.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@335",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@338",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@341",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@345",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@347",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@350"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.06",
            "text": "The constructed model uses the same vocabulary size, 50,257.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@352",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@356",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@358"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.07",
            "text": "For simplicity, one token is treated as one word in the running example.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@669",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@671",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@689",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@691"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.08",
            "text": "That simplification is not how GPT-2 actually tokenizes text, because GPT-2 uses byte pair encoding as a subword tokenizer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@673",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@675",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@678"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p01.09",
            "text": "As a result, one word is not necessarily one token, since tokens can be small subwords or even individual characters.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@683",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@685",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1452",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1454",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1456",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1458"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s02.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.01",
            "text": "The same setup also fixes how much prior text the model can use at once.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.02",
            "text": "The context size is the maximum number of words that can be used to predict the next word.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@853",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@855",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@858",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@860"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.03",
            "text": "In the small example built around the four words \"every effort moves you\", the context size is four, so only four positions matter.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@636",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@698",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@700",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@851",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@853",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@862",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@864"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.04",
            "text": "GPT-2 used a context length of 1024.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2444",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2447",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@363",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@365",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1868",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1871"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.05",
            "text": "Here the model uses a context length of 256 so the code is easier to train locally, can run on a learner's machine in less time, and requires fewer computational resources.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@367",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@370",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@372",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@375",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@377",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1360",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1363",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1366",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1371",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1373",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1376",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1377",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1865",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1871",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1874",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1877",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1879"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.06",
            "text": "The same code can be used with a longer context length if there is enough compute power and memory.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@377",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@380",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@382",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@384"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p02.07",
            "text": "Changing the context length from 256 to 1024 does not significantly change the code.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Zxf-34voZss@1881",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1883",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1885"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s02.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.01",
            "text": "With vocabulary and context fixed, the remaining configuration values describe the model's internal size and structure.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.02",
            "text": "The GPT-2 configuration shown in code sets vocabulary size to 50257, context length to 1024, embedding dimension to 768, number of attention heads to 12, number of transformer blocks to 12, dropout rate to 0.1, and query-key-value bias to false.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2423",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2426",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2434",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2438",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2441",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2444",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2447",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2450",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2452",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2455",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2458",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2461",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2463",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2465",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2468"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.03",
            "text": "The configuration used for the model here keeps vocabulary size 50257, embedding dimension 768, 12 attention heads, 12 transformer layers, dropout 0.1, and query-key-value bias false, while reducing context length to 256.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1290",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1291",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1294",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1296",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1300",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1303",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1305",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1308",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1310",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1862",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1864",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1865",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1885",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1887",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1889",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1893",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1895",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1897",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1900",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1904"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.04",
            "text": "The embedding dimension is the higher-dimensional space into which token embeddings are projected to capture semantic meaning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@387",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@389",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@391",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@393",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@394",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@397"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.05",
            "text": "Here that embedding dimension is 768, because the inputs are projected into a 768-dimensional space.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2447",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2452",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1885",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1887",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1889",
              "Zxf-34voZss@1891"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.06",
            "text": "The number of attention heads is the number of self-attention mechanism blocks within one transformer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@398",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@400",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@402",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@404"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.07",
            "text": "The number of layers specifies how many transformer blocks the model has.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@409",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@411",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@413"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s02.p03.08",
            "text": "In GPT-2, 12 transformer blocks are used, and each transformer's multi-head attention module uses 12 attention heads.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2498",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2501",
              "G3-JgHckzjw@2502",
